    basic: "Dump your 'heavies' early. High doublendtiles (6-6, 5-5) are a massive liability in Gallinazo. If you can't play them early, they will balloon your score if the deal ends suddenly.",
    intermediate: "Pip Management: Toward the end of a deal, try to keep tiles that give you the most matching options (like a 1nd1 or 2nd2) rather than tiles with high pips. A low-value 'dead end' tile can be used to block the game if you are sure you have the lowest score.",
    advanced: 'The Block Trap: If you have a very low pip count (e.g., only a 0-1 and 1nd1 left), you may want to deliberately block the line. In Gallinazo, if you can block the game while holding the lowest count, you force every opponent to take a full point hit.',
    tips: [
      {
        title: 'Tip',
        description: 'If you are the last player to move, be extremely careful. If you block the game and someone else has a tie or lower score, your 4x penalty can end your chances for the entire match.',
      },
      {
        title: 'Tip',
        description: "Pay attention to which suits (pip values) have been played 7 times. This tells you which ends are 'dead' and can be used to block.",
      },
      {
        title: 'Tip',
        description: 'In the 7th deal, play extremely conservatively. This is when the secret scores are revealed and the pressure is highest.',
      },
